<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meuse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maas, River Meuse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meuse (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>: Maas) is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 925 km long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Since Roman times it has been both a trade route and a frontier, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 used it to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, Sedan, Namur, Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> lie on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meuse.jpg" alt="Meuse" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marc Ryckaert · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinant_Meuse_R01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
